Message-ID: <Enh0fmE1-Is.pLGuaFgr36cJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Enh0fmE1-Is.sagr4CRlDPoJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
References: <Enh0fmE1-Is.sagr4CRlDPoJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/Enh0fmE1-Is/m/pLGuaFgr36cJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Using with(), you have to repeat the with() call once and the name of the data frame twice for every new variable you create:

df$newvar1 <- with(df, var1 + var2)
df$newvar2 <- with(df, var3 + var4)
df$newvar3 <- with(df, var5 + var6)

When using mutate(), you only have to name the data frame twice regardless of how many variables you create. I think within() is closer to mutate() than with() is.

df <- mutate(df,
  newvar1 = var1 + var2,
  newvar2 = var3 + var4,
  newvar3 = var5 + var6)

On Tuesday, December 9, 2014 4:37:11 PM UTC-5, Robbie Edwards wrote:

> Why would I choose to use mutate() instead of something like:
>
> df$newvar <-with( df, var1 + var2 )
>
> Or in general, what are use cases for mutate that maybe aren't readily apparent.
>
> robbie
